# Results: does contrastive decision learning help a Mamba encoder trade?

**Short answer: no.** On 14 walk-forward test years (2012–2025, 56 large US stocks),
the Mamba encoder with the contrastive state–action head (D) trades the same as the
same encoder with a plain MLP head (C): Sharpe difference −0.00 [−0.16, +0.15] in the
main run, +0.055 on average over four seeds, with every 95% interval containing zero.
The small positive average comes from lower turnover, not better forecasts: before
costs the average difference is 0.00. No ablation (window 128, d_state 32, 4 layers,
horizon 20, head variants) changes that.

The simpler models do better than any Mamba variant. Linear, LightGBM and the
last-row MLP have the best log loss and the best Sharpe, and all three beat Mamba+MLP
significantly (+0.20 to +0.24 Sharpe, intervals above zero). **No model beats
equal-weight buy-and-hold after costs** (Sharpe 0.99): the best, linear at 0.97, is
level with it at a lower drawdown (27.6% vs 34.3%). Every model's forecasts are barely
better than the class prior (test log loss 1.066–1.074, against 1.077–1.079 for a
constant class-share forecast).

These are negative results and are reported as such. The engineering parts (scan
kernels, leakage-free CV, proofs) are verified and listed in the second half.

Reports used here: [`results/reports/`](results/reports/) (`main.md` is the main
study; `kaggle_*.md` are the replicates and ablations; gzip JSON alongside).

## 1. Hypotheses (fixed before any test fold was looked at)

| | Hypothesis | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| H1 | Mamba+CLM (D) has lower test log loss than Mamba+MLP (C) | **Not supported.** Main 1.0741 vs 1.0733 (D worse); across all 12 paired runs D is lower in 6, tied in 1, worse in 5, never by more than 0.0022 |
| H2 | D has a higher Sharpe after costs than C | **Not supported.** Main −0.00 [−0.16, +0.15]; seeds +0.07, +0.03, +0.12, all intervals include 0 |
| H3 | A Mamba encoder beats non-sequential baselines (linear, LightGBM, MLP on the last row) | **Rejected.** All three beat C significantly (paired Sharpe +0.20 to +0.24; log loss lower in 12–13 of 14 years) |
| H4 | Some model beats buy-and-hold after costs | **Not supported.** Best Sharpe 0.97 vs 0.99 |
| H5 | SupCon (E) or auxiliary return/risk heads (F) help CLM | E is the best Mamba variant (Sharpe 0.85, log loss 1.0692; +0.13 [−0.03, +0.29] vs C, not significant); F +0.05 [−0.11, +0.19] |

## 2. Setup

**Data.** Yahoo daily bars 1999-01-04 … 2026-10-21 (6,993 trading days) for 56 large
US stocks (the traded universe), plus SPY and the 9 sector SPDRs as market and sector
context. `tools/fetch_yahoo.py` downloads them; `universe.json` holds the list.

**Features** (C2 in [`docs/CONTRACTS.md`](docs/CONTRACTS.md)): 20 point-in-time
features per stock and day: log returns over 1/5/20/60 days, overnight gap,
intraday return, high–low range, realised volatility (20/60 days), relative volume,
momentum, distance to the 52-week high, Amihud illiquidity, market return and
volatility, sector-relative returns, and cross-sectional z-scores. An audit recomputes every feature on bars truncated at each date
and finds 0 mismatches on the real data, so no feature reads the future.

**Labels** (C3): target exposures over h = 5 days, executed at the next open. BUY if
the forward return is above θ = 2κ + 0.5·σ_h, SELL if below −θ, otherwise HOLD, with
κ = 5 bp one-way cost. The class shares are 32 / 26 / 42% (124,230 / 101,055 / 164,867
training-pool samples). The label does not depend on the current position; the policy
in C5 adds that.

**Folds** (C4): expanding window with one test year per fold, 2012 … 2025. Each fold
has train (from 1999), validation (252 days), calibration (252 days) and test (one
year). A sample is kept only if its label window ends before the next segment starts
plus a 5-day embargo (`t + 1 + h + e < s_next`), which purges 11 days at each
boundary. Before training, every fold runs `check_no_leakage` on the actual sample
indices. Normalisation statistics come from the fold's training days only.

**Training and tuning budget, identical for every learned model**: AdamW, cosine
schedule with 100 warm-up steps, at most 2,000 steps of batch 256, gradient clipping
at 1, early stopping on validation log loss (patience 3 evaluations of 200 steps),
and two candidate settings chosen on validation log loss. LightGBM also gets two
candidate settings (its own), chosen the same way on the same exported folds. Each prediction is temperature-calibrated on
the calibration year.

**Models** (main study, window 64):

| | Model | Parameters |
|---|---|---|
| linear | multinomial logistic regression on the last row | 63 |
| lgbm | LightGBM on the last row (external, Python, same folds) | – |
| A | MLP on the last row | 5,699 |
| B | TCN (4 layers, d 64) + MLP head | 23,939 |
| C | Mamba-1 (2 layers, d 64, d_state 16, expand 2) + MLP head — reference | 71,171 |
| D | same encoder + contrastive state/action head (InfoNCE, learned temperature) | 79,745 |
| E | D + supervised contrastive loss between states (weight 0.5) | 79,745 |
| F | D + auxiliary return and risk heads (weights 0.5 / 0.2) | 88,195 |

**Policy and accounting** (C5): edge = p_buy − p_sell. Go long if edge > τ, go flat
if edge < −τ, otherwise keep the position. Positions are equal weight across the 56
stocks and trade at the next open with 5 bp one-way cost. The primary policy uses
τ = 0 for every model, fixed in advance. A secondary policy tunes τ on the
calibration year. Statistics (C6): stationary block bootstrap with mean block 20 days
and 2,000 resamples, paired by day for model differences.

## 3. Main study (`results/reports/main.md`)

All 8 models finished all 14 folds, so every table covers the same 197,120 test
samples over 3,520 days.

### Forecast quality (test, temperature-calibrated)

| model | log loss | Brier | ECE | macro AUC | IC mean (t) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| linear | 1.0675 | 0.6446 | 0.011 | 0.559 | 0.0073 (1.99) |
| lgbm | 1.0680 | 0.6452 | 0.005 | 0.556 | 0.0020 (0.57) |
| **A_mlp** | **1.0663** | **0.6435** | 0.004 | **0.564** | **0.0092 (2.56)** |
| B_tcn_mlp | 1.0708 | 0.6468 | 0.004 | 0.551 | 0.0005 (0.15) |
| C_mamba_mlp | 1.0733 | 0.6487 | 0.004 | 0.551 | 0.0044 (1.34) |
| D_mamba_clm | 1.0741 | 0.6490 | **0.002** | 0.545 | 0.0036 (1.14) |
| E_mamba_supcon | 1.0692 | 0.6458 | 0.003 | 0.555 | 0.0043 (1.31) |
| F_mamba_clm_aux | 1.0731 | 0.6485 | 0.005 | 0.546 | 0.0063 (1.94) |

The signal is very weak for every model. The sequence models (B–F) see 64 days of
history but forecast worse than models that see only the last day. D is the
best-calibrated model (ECE 0.002), but its ranking (AUC) is the weakest.

**Calibration.** Temperature scaling gives the lowest ECE for every model. Vector
scaling and isotonic regression, fitted on one year, overfit it: isotonic raises test
log loss by 0.01–0.02 for every model. For the linear model and LightGBM even
temperature scaling raises log loss slightly (1.0658 → 1.0675). Their raw outputs
were already about as well calibrated as one year of data can tell.

### Trading after costs (primary policy, τ = 0, 5 bp one way)

| model | ann. return | Sharpe [95% CI] | max DD | turnover/day | long share |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| linear | 12.6% | 0.97 [0.47, 1.52] | 27.6% | 0.113 | 87% |
| lgbm | 12.6% | 0.96 [0.48, 1.47] | 25.2% | 0.123 | 86% |
| A_mlp | 11.5% | 0.92 [0.42, 1.48] | 28.4% | 0.143 | 84% |
| B_tcn_mlp | 9.7% | 0.81 [0.30, 1.35] | 27.0% | 0.195 | 77% |
| C_mamba_mlp | 8.2% | 0.72 [0.24, 1.27] | 25.8% | 0.259 | 70% |
| D_mamba_clm | 8.7% | 0.72 [0.23, 1.26] | 28.0% | 0.205 | 76% |
| E_mamba_supcon | 10.8% | 0.85 [0.34, 1.39] | 29.1% | 0.143 | 81% |
| F_mamba_clm_aux | 9.2% | 0.77 [0.28, 1.30] | 27.7% | 0.204 | 75% |
| buy & hold, equal weight | 15.1% | 0.99 | 34.3% | 0.004 | 100% |

All models are long most of the time. The question is whether stepping aside on SELL
days avoids more losses than it gives up in gains and costs. The ranking follows
turnover almost exactly: the more a model trades, the worse it does. Before costs
(0 bp), linear, LightGBM and the MLP reach Sharpe 1.07–1.08, slightly above
buy-and-hold. The 5 bp costs take that away. At 20 bp, Mamba+MLP's Sharpe is
negative (−0.15).

Tuning τ on the calibration year (secondary policy) cuts turnover by 3–6× but does
not help consistently. It raises C to 0.88 and lowers linear to 0.66. One
calibration year is too noisy to pick a threshold.

### Paired comparison with Mamba+MLP (C)

| model | Sharpe diff [95% CI] | mean daily diff, bp [95% CI] | years better Sharpe | years lower log loss |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| linear | **+0.24 [+0.11, +0.38]** | +1.77 [+1.09, +2.50] | 12/14 | 12/14 |
| lgbm | **+0.23 [+0.05, +0.41]** | +1.75 [+0.82, +2.80] | 9/14 | 12/14 |
| A_mlp | **+0.20 [+0.07, +0.33]** | +1.33 [+0.70, +2.00] | 11/14 | 13/14 |
| E_mamba_supcon | +0.13 [−0.03, +0.29] | +1.06 [+0.25, +1.83] | 11/14 | 13/14 |
| B_tcn_mlp | +0.08 [−0.06, +0.23] | +0.62 [−0.04, +1.35] | 8/14 | 11/14 |
| F_mamba_clm_aux | +0.05 [−0.11, +0.19] | +0.43 [−0.26, +1.14] | 7/14 | 6/14 |
| D_mamba_clm | −0.00 [−0.16, +0.15] | +0.23 [−0.50, +0.96] | 6/14 | 4/14 |

These intervals are not corrected for the seven comparisons. Even so, the conclusion
does not depend on that: the three significant differences favour the simple models.

## 4. Seed replicates of the key comparison (D vs C)

The same config was rerun with three other model seeds (`replicate_seed{1,2,3}.toml`)
on Kaggle T4 GPUs (CUDA backend). The folds, data and costs are identical.

| seed | log loss C / D | Sharpe C / D | D − C Sharpe [95% CI] | D − C at 0 bp | turnover C / D |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| main | 1.0733 / 1.0741 | 0.72 / 0.72 | −0.00 [−0.16, +0.15] | −0.08 | 0.259 / 0.205 |
| 1 | 1.0722 / 1.0716 | 0.73 / 0.79 | +0.07 [−0.05, +0.21] | +0.02 | 0.250 / 0.215 |
| 2 | 1.0709 / 1.0710 | 0.78 / 0.81 | +0.03 [−0.09, +0.14] | +0.02 | 0.233 / 0.223 |
| 3 | 1.0723 / 1.0701 | 0.65 / 0.77 | +0.12 [−0.01, +0.26] | +0.06 | 0.257 / 0.223 |
| mean | 1.0722 / 1.0717 | 0.72 / 0.77 | +0.055 | +0.005 | 0.250 / 0.217 |

The seed alone moves C's Sharpe between 0.65 and 0.78, which is as large as any D − C
difference. D's one consistent property is that it trades about 13% less. Its log loss
is no better.

## 5. Ablations (each against its own Mamba+MLP twin; Kaggle T4)

| ablation (config) | C Sharpe | D Sharpe | D − C [95% CI] | log loss C / D |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| window 128 (`window128.toml`) | 0.77 | 0.78 | +0.01 [−0.10, +0.14] | 1.0730 / 1.0730 |
| d_state 32 (`dstate32.toml`) | 0.79 | 0.84 | +0.05 [−0.07, +0.18] | 1.0725 / 1.0714 |
| 4 layers (`layers4.toml`) | 0.75 | 0.72 | −0.03 [−0.21, +0.12] | 1.0736 / 1.0739 |
| horizon 20 (`horizon20.toml`) | 0.80 | 0.79 | −0.01 [−0.09, +0.08] | 1.0799 / 1.0780 |

Contrastive-head variants (`clm_head.toml`), each against the main config's C (Sharpe 0.72):

| variant | Sharpe | vs C [95% CI] | log loss |
|---|---|---|---|
| fixed temperature 0.1 | 0.70 | −0.02 [−0.18, +0.13] | 1.0744 |
| embedding width 16 | 0.80 | +0.07 [−0.08, +0.23] | 1.0728 |
| bilinear scoring | 0.67 | −0.05 [−0.20, +0.09] | 1.0739 |
| multi-positive (ambiguity band 0.25) | 0.79 | +0.06 [−0.07, +0.20] | 1.0723 |

More context, more state, more depth and a longer horizon do not help either head.
None of the head variants is significantly different from C.

Reproducibility check: the `clm_head` run on CUDA rebuilt C with the main study's
seed. It matches the main run on the local Radeon (wgpu) to four decimals in every
metric (log loss 1.0743 → 1.0733, Sharpe 0.72 [0.24, 1.27], turnover 0.259), so the
CUDA and wgpu backends train the same model.

## 6. What the evidence supports

* Contrastive state–action learning does not improve this Mamba encoder's forecasts
  or its trading, in any of 12 paired comparisons with its MLP twin (4 seeds, 4
  ablations, 4 head variants). It makes the model trade a little
  less, which is worth a few hundredths of Sharpe at 5 bp.
* The Mamba encoder itself is the weak part. With identical tuning budgets, a
  logistic regression, LightGBM or a small MLP on the last day's features forecast
  and trade better. A 64-day sequence model mostly finds noise to fit in this
  low-signal setting (best test log loss 0.011 below a constant class-share forecast).
* The pipeline is not "broken in the model's favour". On a synthetic panel with a
  planted signal the same code learns it (clm-data `synthetic`, clm-model
  learnability test), and the before-cost Sharpe of the simple models sits slightly
  above buy-and-hold, which is what a weak, real signal would look like.

## 7. Engineering verification

**Selective-scan kernels** (core crate, `src/tensor/ops/selective_scan.rs`): forward
(sequential and three-phase chunked associative scan) and a checkpointed backward,
in CubeCL. They are checked against the `f64` reference in `src/ssm/selective.rs`
(finite-difference gradients, causality) by `tests/selective_scan.rs` on the CPU
runtime, wgpu (Radeon 860M) and CUDA (T4). These include half-precision storage
(f16 on wgpu; this GPU does not support bf16).

**Lean 4** (`lean/ClmVerify`, about 1,850 lines, 71 main theorems plus supporting
lemmas, 126 in all; no `sorry`, no added axioms, no `native_decide`). The proofs cover:

* scan associativity and chunked scan = sequential scan;
* causality, and the adjoint identity of the backward;
* checkpointed states = sequential states;
* fold/purge/embargo disjointness and the keep rule;
* label windows;
* backtest accounting.

The Rust implementation is *tested* against the Lean executable `clmcheck`
(differential cases in clm-eval), not proven. The Lean model does not cover the GPU
backward's memory layout. See `lean/ClmVerify/REPORT.md`.

**Tests**:

* 37 clm-data tests. Injected leaks must be caught: a feature that reads the next
  close, a full-sample mean, or a future cross-section.
* 88 clm-eval tests, including the Lean oracle and the purging guard. The guard must
  reject a training label that overlaps validation, an embargo violation, and a
  calibration label that reaches the test year.
* 8 clm-model tests: the encoder against the `f64` reference in both scan schedules,
  causality, finite-difference gradients, checkpoint round trip, learning a planted
  signal, fixed-seed reproducibility, and every model overfitting a small batch.
* 8 scan tests.

MODEL_TEST_STATUS

**Reviews**: four rounds of codex review (`docs/reviews/`). All findings were fixed,
except one that was documented on purpose (the CLM action attribute uses the constant
2κ threshold).

**Leakage controls in the runner**:

* `cv` refuses to resume when the config, data hash or source hash changes.
* `report` refuses a run whose `run.json` signature does not match.
* External (LightGBM) predictions must match the fold manifest: data, split keys and
  normaliser hash, with exact key counts.
