exec
/bin/bash -lc "nl -ba RESULTS.md | sed -n '1,360p'; for f in results/reports/kaggle_{seed1,seed2,seed3,w128,n32,l4,h20,head}.md; do echo \"===== "'$f"; rg -n "''^(#|##)|'"\\| (C_|D_|buy)|\\| fixed|\\| embedding|\\| bilinear|\\| multi|\\| linear|\\| lgbm|\\| A_mlp\" \""'$f"; done' in /home/user/Documents/workspace/mamba-trainer/finance
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Results: does contrastive decision learning help a Mamba encoder trade?
     2	
     3	**Short answer: no.** On 14 walk-forward test years (2012–2025, 56 large US stocks),
     4	the Mamba encoder with the contrastive state–action head (D) trades the same as the
     5	same encoder with a plain MLP head (C): Sharpe difference −0.00 [−0.16, +0.15] in the
     6	main run, +0.055 on average over four seeds, with every 95% interval containing zero.
     7	The small positive average comes from lower turnover, not better forecasts: before
     8	costs the average difference is 0.00. No ablation (window 128, d_state 32, 4 layers,
     9	horizon 20, head variants) changes that.
    10	
    11	The simpler models do better than any Mamba variant. Linear, LightGBM and the
    12	last-row MLP have the best log loss and the best Sharpe, and all three beat Mamba+MLP
    13	significantly (+0.20 to +0.24 Sharpe, intervals above zero). **No model beats
    14	equal-weight buy-and-hold after costs** (Sharpe 0.99): the best, linear at 0.97, is
    15	level with it at a lower drawdown (27.6% vs 34.3%). Every model's forecasts are barely
    16	better than the class prior (test log loss 1.066–1.074, against 1.077–1.079 for a
    17	constant class-share forecast).
    18	
    19	These are negative results and are reported as such. The engineering parts (scan
    20	kernels, leakage-free CV, proofs) are verified and listed in the second half.
    21	
    22	Reports used here: [`results/reports/`](results/reports/) (`main.md` is the main
    23	study; `kaggle_*.md` are the replicates and ablations; gzip JSON alongside).
    24	
    25	## 1. Hypotheses (fixed before any test fold was looked at)
    26	
    27	| | Hypothesis | Verdict |
    28	|---|---|---|
    29	| H1 | Mamba+CLM (D) has lower test log loss than Mamba+MLP (C) | **Not supported.** Main 1.0741 vs 1.0733 (D worse); across all 12 paired runs D is lower in 6, tied in 1, worse in 5, never by more than 0.0022 |
    30	| H2 | D has a higher Sharpe after costs than C | **Not supported.** Main −0.00 [−0.16, +0.15]; seeds +0.07, +0.03, +0.12, all intervals include 0 |
    31	| H3 | A Mamba encoder beats non-sequential baselines (linear, LightGBM, MLP on the last row) | **Rejected.** All three beat C significantly (paired Sharpe +0.20 to +0.24; log loss lower in 12–13 of 14 years) |
    32	| H4 | Some model beats buy-and-hold after costs | **Not supported.** Best Sharpe 0.97 vs 0.99 |
    33	| H5 | SupCon (E) or auxiliary return/risk heads (F) help CLM | E is the best Mamba variant (Sharpe 0.85, log loss 1.0692; +0.13 [−0.03, +0.29] vs C, not significant); F +0.05 [−0.11, +0.19] |
    34	
    35	## 2. Setup
    36	
    37	**Data.** Yahoo daily bars 1999-01-04 … 2026-10-21 (6,993 trading days) for 56 large
    38	US stocks (the traded universe), plus SPY and the 9 sector SPDRs as market and sector
    39	context. `tools/fetch_yahoo.py` downloads them; `universe.json` holds the list.
    40	
    41	**Features** (C2 in [`docs/CONTRACTS.md`](docs/CONTRACTS.md)): 20 point-in-time
    42	features per stock and day: log returns over 1/5/20/60 days, overnight gap,
    43	intraday return, high–low range, realised volatility (20/60 days), relative volume,
    44	momentum, distance to the 52-week high, Amihud illiquidity, market return and
    45	volatility, sector-relative returns, and cross-sectional z-scores. An audit recomputes every feature on bars truncated at each date
    46	and finds 0 mismatches on the real data, so no feature reads the future.
    47	
    48	**Labels** (C3): target exposures over h = 5 days, executed at the next open. BUY if
    49	the forward return is above θ = 2κ + 0.5·σ_h, SELL if below −θ, otherwise HOLD, with
    50	κ = 5 bp one-way cost. The class shares are 32 / 26 / 42% (124,230 / 101,055 / 164,867
    51	training-pool samples). The label does not depend on the current position; the policy
    52	in C5 adds that.
    53	
    54	**Folds** (C4): expanding window with one test year per fold, 2012 … 2025. Each fold
    55	has train (from 1999), validation (252 days), calibration (252 days) and test (one
    56	year). A sample is kept only if its label window ends before the next segment starts
    57	plus a 5-day embargo (`t + 1 + h + e < s_next`), which purges 11 days at each
    58	boundary. Before training, every fold runs `check_no_leakage` on the actual sample
    59	indices. Normalisation statistics come from the fold's training days only.
    60	
    61	**Training and tuning budget, identical for every learned model**: AdamW, cosine
    62	schedule with 100 warm-up steps, at most 2,000 steps of batch 256, gradient clipping
    63	at 1, early stopping on validation log loss (patience 3 evaluations of 200 steps),
    64	and two candidate settings chosen on validation log loss. LightGBM also gets two
    65	candidate settings (its own), chosen the same way on the same exported folds. Each prediction is temperature-calibrated on
    66	the calibration year.
    67	
    68	**Models** (main study, window 64):
    69	
    70	| | Model | Parameters |
    71	|---|---|---|
    72	| linear | multinomial logistic regression on the last row | 63 |
    73	| lgbm | LightGBM on the last row (external, Python, same folds) | – |
    74	| A | MLP on the last row | 5,699 |
    75	| B | TCN (4 layers, d 64) + MLP head | 23,939 |
    76	| C | Mamba-1 (2 layers, d 64, d_state 16, expand 2) + MLP head — reference | 71,171 |
    77	| D | same encoder + contrastive state/action head (InfoNCE, learned temperature) | 79,745 |
    78	| E | D + supervised contrastive loss between states (weight 0.5) | 79,745 |
    79	| F | D + auxiliary return and risk heads (weights 0.5 / 0.2) | 88,195 |
    80	
    81	**Policy and accounting** (C5): edge = p_buy − p_sell. Go long if edge > τ, go flat
    82	if edge < −τ, otherwise keep the position. Positions are equal weight across the 56
    83	stocks and trade at the next open with 5 bp one-way cost. The primary policy uses
    84	τ = 0 for every model, fixed in advance. A secondary policy tunes τ on the
    85	calibration year. Statistics (C6): stationary block bootstrap with mean block 20 days
    86	and 2,000 resamples, paired by day for model differences.
    87	
    88	## 3. Main study (`results/reports/main.md`)
    89	
    90	All 8 models finished all 14 folds, so every table covers the same 197,120 test
    91	samples over 3,520 days.
    92	
    93	### Forecast quality (test, temperature-calibrated)
    94	
    95	| model | log loss | Brier | ECE | macro AUC | IC mean (t) |
    96	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
    97	| linear | 1.0675 | 0.6446 | 0.011 | 0.559 | 0.0073 (1.99) |
    98	| lgbm | 1.0680 | 0.6452 | 0.005 | 0.556 | 0.0020 (0.57) |
    99	| **A_mlp** | **1.0663** | **0.6435** | 0.004 | **0.564** | **0.0092 (2.56)** |
   100	| B_tcn_mlp | 1.0708 | 0.6468 | 0.004 | 0.551 | 0.0005 (0.15) |
   101	| C_mamba_mlp | 1.0733 | 0.6487 | 0.004 | 0.551 | 0.0044 (1.34) |
   102	| D_mamba_clm | 1.0741 | 0.6490 | **0.002** | 0.545 | 0.0036 (1.14) |
   103	| E_mamba_supcon | 1.0692 | 0.6458 | 0.003 | 0.555 | 0.0043 (1.31) |
   104	| F_mamba_clm_aux | 1.0731 | 0.6485 | 0.005 | 0.546 | 0.0063 (1.94) |
   105	
   106	The signal is very weak for every model. The sequence models (B–F) see 64 days of
   107	history but forecast worse than models that see only the last day. D is the
   108	best-calibrated model (ECE 0.002), but its ranking (AUC) is the weakest.
   109	
   110	**Calibration.** Temperature scaling gives the lowest ECE for every model. Vector
   111	scaling and isotonic regression, fitted on one year, overfit it: isotonic raises test
   112	log loss by 0.01–0.02 for every model. For the linear model and LightGBM even
   113	temperature scaling raises log loss slightly (1.0658 → 1.0675). Their raw outputs
   114	were already about as well calibrated as one year of data can tell.
   115	
   116	### Trading after costs (primary policy, τ = 0, 5 bp one way)
   117	
   118	| model | ann. return | Sharpe [95% CI] | max DD | turnover/day | long share |
   119	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   120	| linear | 12.6% | 0.97 [0.47, 1.52] | 27.6% | 0.113 | 87% |
   121	| lgbm | 12.6% | 0.96 [0.48, 1.47] | 25.2% | 0.123 | 86% |
   122	| A_mlp | 11.5% | 0.92 [0.42, 1.48] | 28.4% | 0.143 | 84% |
   123	| B_tcn_mlp | 9.7% | 0.81 [0.30, 1.35] | 27.0% | 0.195 | 77% |
   124	| C_mamba_mlp | 8.2% | 0.72 [0.24, 1.27] | 25.8% | 0.259 | 70% |
   125	| D_mamba_clm | 8.7% | 0.72 [0.23, 1.26] | 28.0% | 0.205 | 76% |
   126	| E_mamba_supcon | 10.8% | 0.85 [0.34, 1.39] | 29.1% | 0.143 | 81% |
   127	| F_mamba_clm_aux | 9.2% | 0.77 [0.28, 1.30] | 27.7% | 0.204 | 75% |
   128	| buy & hold, equal weight | 15.1% | 0.99 | 34.3% | 0.004 | 100% |
   129	
   130	All models are long most of the time. The question is whether stepping aside on SELL
   131	days avoids more losses than it gives up in gains and costs. The ranking follows
   132	turnover almost exactly: the more a model trades, the worse it does. Before costs
   133	(0 bp), linear, LightGBM and the MLP reach Sharpe 1.07–1.08, slightly above
   134	buy-and-hold. The 5 bp costs take that away. At 20 bp, Mamba+MLP's Sharpe is
   135	negative (−0.15).
   136	
   137	Tuning τ on the calibration year (secondary policy) cuts turnover by 3–6× but does
   138	not help consistently. It raises C to 0.88 and lowers linear to 0.66. One
   139	calibration year is too noisy to pick a threshold.
   140	
   141	### Paired comparison with Mamba+MLP (C)
   142	
   143	| model | Sharpe diff [95% CI] | mean daily diff, bp [95% CI] | years better Sharpe | years lower log loss |
   144	|---|---|---|---|---|
   145	| linear | **+0.24 [+0.11, +0.38]** | +1.77 [+1.09, +2.50] | 12/14 | 12/14 |
   146	| lgbm | **+0.23 [+0.05, +0.41]** | +1.75 [+0.82, +2.80] | 9/14 | 12/14 |
   147	| A_mlp | **+0.20 [+0.07, +0.33]** | +1.33 [+0.70, +2.00] | 11/14 | 13/14 |
   148	| E_mamba_supcon | +0.13 [−0.03, +0.29] | +1.06 [+0.25, +1.83] | 11/14 | 13/14 |
   149	| B_tcn_mlp | +0.08 [−0.06, +0.23] | +0.62 [−0.04, +1.35] | 8/14 | 11/14 |
   150	| F_mamba_clm_aux | +0.05 [−0.11, +0.19] | +0.43 [−0.26, +1.14] | 7/14 | 6/14 |
   151	| D_mamba_clm | −0.00 [−0.16, +0.15] | +0.23 [−0.50, +0.96] | 6/14 | 4/14 |
   152	
   153	These intervals are not corrected for the seven comparisons. Even so, the conclusion
   154	does not depend on that: the three significant differences favour the simple models.
   155	
   156	## 4. Seed replicates of the key comparison (D vs C)
   157	
   158	The same config was rerun with three other model seeds (`replicate_seed{1,2,3}.toml`)
   159	on Kaggle T4 GPUs (CUDA backend). The folds, data and costs are identical.
   160	
   161	| seed | log loss C / D | Sharpe C / D | D − C Sharpe [95% CI] | D − C at 0 bp | turnover C / D |
   162	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   163	| main | 1.0733 / 1.0741 | 0.72 / 0.72 | −0.00 [−0.16, +0.15] | −0.08 | 0.259 / 0.205 |
   164	| 1 | 1.0722 / 1.0716 | 0.73 / 0.79 | +0.07 [−0.05, +0.21] | +0.02 | 0.250 / 0.215 |
   165	| 2 | 1.0709 / 1.0710 | 0.78 / 0.81 | +0.03 [−0.09, +0.14] | +0.02 | 0.233 / 0.223 |
   166	| 3 | 1.0723 / 1.0701 | 0.65 / 0.77 | +0.12 [−0.01, +0.26] | +0.06 | 0.257 / 0.223 |
   167	| mean | 1.0722 / 1.0717 | 0.72 / 0.77 | +0.055 | +0.005 | 0.250 / 0.217 |
   168	
   169	The seed alone moves C's Sharpe between 0.65 and 0.78, which is as large as any D − C
   170	difference. D's one consistent property is that it trades about 13% less. Its log loss
   171	is no better.
   172	
   173	## 5. Ablations (each against its own Mamba+MLP twin; Kaggle T4)
   174	
   175	| ablation (config) | C Sharpe | D Sharpe | D − C [95% CI] | log loss C / D |
   176	|---|---|---|---|---|
   177	| window 128 (`window128.toml`) | 0.77 | 0.78 | +0.01 [−0.10, +0.14] | 1.0730 / 1.0730 |
   178	| d_state 32 (`dstate32.toml`) | 0.79 | 0.84 | +0.05 [−0.07, +0.18] | 1.0725 / 1.0714 |
   179	| 4 layers (`layers4.toml`) | 0.75 | 0.72 | −0.03 [−0.21, +0.12] | 1.0736 / 1.0739 |
   180	| horizon 20 (`horizon20.toml`) | 0.80 | 0.79 | −0.01 [−0.09, +0.08] | 1.0799 / 1.0780 |
   181	
   182	Contrastive-head variants (`clm_head.toml`), each against the main config's C (Sharpe 0.72):
   183	
   184	| variant | Sharpe | vs C [95% CI] | log loss |
   185	|---|---|---|---|
   186	| fixed temperature 0.1 | 0.70 | −0.02 [−0.18, +0.13] | 1.0744 |
   187	| embedding width 16 | 0.80 | +0.07 [−0.08, +0.23] | 1.0728 |
   188	| bilinear scoring | 0.67 | −0.05 [−0.20, +0.09] | 1.0739 |
   189	| multi-positive (ambiguity band 0.25) | 0.79 | +0.06 [−0.07, +0.20] | 1.0723 |
   190	
   191	More context, more state, more depth and a longer horizon do not help either head.
   192	None of the head variants is significantly different from C.
   193	
   194	Reproducibility check: the `clm_head` run on CUDA rebuilt C with the main study's
   195	seed. It matches the main run on the local Radeon (wgpu) to four decimals in every
   196	metric (log loss 1.0743 → 1.0733, Sharpe 0.72 [0.24, 1.27], turnover 0.259), so the
   197	CUDA and wgpu backends train the same model.
   198	
   199	## 6. What the evidence supports
   200	
   201	* Contrastive state–action learning does not improve this Mamba encoder's forecasts
   202	  or its trading, in any of 12 paired comparisons with its MLP twin (4 seeds, 4
   203	  ablations, 4 head variants). It makes the model trade a little
   204	  less, which is worth a few hundredths of Sharpe at 5 bp.
   205	* The Mamba encoder itself is the weak part. With identical tuning budgets, a
   206	  logistic regression, LightGBM or a small MLP on the last day's features forecast
   207	  and trade better. A 64-day sequence model mostly finds noise to fit in this
   208	  low-signal setting (best test log loss 0.011 below a constant class-share forecast).
   209	* The pipeline is not "broken in the model's favour". On a synthetic panel with a
   210	  planted signal the same code learns it (clm-data `synthetic`, clm-model
   211	  learnability test), and the before-cost Sharpe of the simple models sits slightly
   212	  above buy-and-hold, which is what a weak, real signal would look like.
   213	
   214	## 7. Engineering verification
   215	
   216	**Selective-scan kernels** (core crate, `src/tensor/ops/selective_scan.rs`): forward
   217	(sequential and three-phase chunked associative scan) and a checkpointed backward,
   218	in CubeCL. They are checked against the `f64` reference in `src/ssm/selective.rs`
   219	(finite-difference gradients, causality) by `tests/selective_scan.rs` on the CPU
   220	runtime, wgpu (Radeon 860M) and CUDA (T4). These include half-precision storage
   221	(f16 on wgpu; this GPU does not support bf16).
   222	
   223	**Lean 4** (`lean/ClmVerify`, about 1,850 lines, 71 main theorems plus supporting
   224	lemmas, 126 in all; no `sorry`, no added axioms, no `native_decide`). The proofs cover:
   225	
   226	* scan associativity and chunked scan = sequential scan;
   227	* causality, and the adjoint identity of the backward;
   228	* checkpointed states = sequential states;
   229	* fold/purge/embargo disjointness and the keep rule;
   230	* label windows;
   231	* backtest accounting.
   232	
   233	The Rust implementation is *tested* against the Lean executable `clmcheck`
   234	(differential cases in clm-eval), not proven. The Lean model does not cover the GPU
   235	backward's memory layout. See `lean/ClmVerify/REPORT.md`.
   236	
   237	**Tests**:
   238	
   239	* 37 clm-data tests. Injected leaks must be caught: a feature that reads the next
   240	  close, a full-sample mean, or a future cross-section.
   241	* 88 clm-eval tests, including the Lean oracle and the purging guard. The guard must
   242	  reject a training label that overlaps validation, an embargo violation, and a
   243	  calibration label that reaches the test year.
   244	* 8 clm-model tests: the encoder against the `f64` reference in both scan schedules,
   245	  causality, finite-difference gradients, checkpoint round trip, learning a planted
   246	  signal, fixed-seed reproducibility, and every model overfitting a small batch.
   247	* 8 scan tests.
   248	
   249	MODEL_TEST_STATUS
   250	
   251	**Reviews**: four rounds of codex review (`docs/reviews/`). All findings were fixed,
   252	except one that was documented on purpose (the CLM action attribute uses the constant
   253	2κ threshold).
   254	
   255	**Leakage controls in the runner**:
   256	
   257	* `cv` refuses to resume when the config, data hash or source hash changes.
   258	* `report` refuses a run whose `run.json` signature does not match.
   259	* External (LightGBM) predictions must match the fold manifest: data, split keys and
   260	  normaliser hash, with exact key counts.
   261	
   262	### Speed
   263	
   264	BENCH_PLACEHOLDER
   265	
   266	## 8. Limitations
   267	
   268	* **Survivorship bias**: the universe is today's large caps, back-filled to 1999.
   269	  This flatters buy-and-hold and every long-biased model alike, so the comparison
   270	  between models is fairer than the absolute returns.
   271	* Static sector map; no corporate-action checks beyond Yahoo's adjusted prices; one
   272	  data source.
   273	* One inner split per fold: one validation year to choose among two candidates and
   274	  one calibration year. Small tuning budgets are deliberate, since bigger ones would
   275	  favour the bigger models, but they leave some performance unexplored.
   276	* The action set has three actions, so "hard negatives" are just the other two
   277	  actions. The plan's quantile heads were not built; the auxiliary heads predict the
   278	  clipped volatility-scaled return and whether the 5% stop is hit.
   279	* Paired intervals are not adjusted for multiple comparisons, and the 5 bp cost model
   280	  has no market impact or borrowing.
   281	* The batch runner varies sequence length only between batches; there is no padding
   282	  mask inside a batch.
   283	* There are no Python bindings for this module, so the repository's Python/Rust
   284	  parity tests do not apply.
